INT. KABIR'S WORKSHOP - DAY

Kabir sits at a wooden desk. He is ten years old, with short cropped hair. He wears a green bomber jacket over a white tee and cargo shorts.

A half-built toy crane stands before him. It is made of gray steel beams. 

He tries to fit a plastic gear onto the main drive shaft. The plastic teeth strip. The gear slips. 

Kabir sighs. He reaches into his canvas satchel. It is covered in colorful patches. He pulls out a folding wooden ruler. 

Click. Click. Click. 

He folds it into a triangle, then a square. The rhythmic sound calms him. 

He looks at the instruction manual. The page is filled with blocks of text. The words look like a dense forest. 

He runs his finger under a sentence. The letters seem to drift. He tries to connect the sounds. 

KABIR
(whispering)
S-p... s-p-u-r... spur. G-e... gear. 

He shakes his head. He closes the manual. He does not need the words. He looks at the 3D diagram. He sees the shapes clearly in his mind. 

KABIR
Plastic is too soft. I need brass. Real brass. 

Leo enters. He is ten, energetic, wearing a bright yellow sweater and blue sneakers. He carries a library book.

LEO
Did you find it? 

KABIR
No. The manual says the vintage kits used metal. But they do not make them anymore. 

LEO
My granddad said the old school time capsule has one. The Class of 1954 buried a golden engineering set. 

KABIR
Where is the capsule?

LEO
In the old tunnels. Under the gym. But the entrance is locked. And nobody has the map. 

KABIR
We need to find that map. 

INT. SCHOOL LIBRARY - DAY

The library is quiet. Sunlight filters through high windows, casting long shadows across the carpet. 

Kabir and Leo stand by the local history shelf. Kabir holds his folding ruler. 

Click. Click. 

He scans the spines of the books. The letters on the narrow labels jump around. He cannot match the written codes to the shelf guide. 

KABIR
Leo. Read the labels for me. Look for "History of Green Valley School."

LEO
On it. 

Leo scans the shelf quickly. He pulls out a thick blue book. 

LEO
Here. "Green Valley: The First Fifty Years."

Leo flips through the pages. A folded piece of yellowed paper falls out. It hits the carpet. 

Kabir picks it up. He unfolds it carefully. 

It is a blueprint. The lines are faded white on blue. It shows the basement of the school. 

But the text on the map is strange. Words overlap. Letters are printed on top of other letters. It looks like a printing error.

LEO
This is a mess. It is misprinted. You can't read any of the room names. 

Kabir stares at the map. His eyes widen. He does not see a mess. He sees layers. 

KABIR
It is not a misprint. Look at the corners. There are register marks. 

LEO
Register what?

KABIR
Marks to align pages. The letters are written in a circle. They are like gears. If you turn them, they line up. 

LEO
I just see alphabet soup. 

INT. CLASSROOM - DAY

Ms. Gable, a kind teacher with glasses, stands by the chalkboard. She writes the word "STRUGGLE" on the board. 

MS. GABLE
Now, who would like to read the next paragraph on page forty-two?

She looks around the room. Kabir lowers his head. He hopes he will not be called on. He assumes he will be last. 

MS. GABLE
Kabir. Would you like to try?

Kabir swallows hard. He opens his textbook. His hands shake slightly. He reaches into his pocket and touches his folding ruler. 

He clears his throat. He looks at the first word. 

KABIR
The... the children... 

He stops. The next word is "stretched." 

He tries to break down the sounds. He connects the "s" and the "t". But the "r" and the "e" get tangled. 

KABIR
S-t-r... ster... 

He feels the heat rising in his cheeks. He knows the word in his head, but his brain struggles to map the letters to the speech sounds. 

KABIR
S-t-r-e... streek...

LEO
(whispering)
Stretched. 

MS. GABLE
Thank you, Leo, but let Kabir try. Kabir, take your time. Use your guide. 

Kabir pulls out a small plastic card with a slot cut in it. He places it over the line. It isolates the words. 

KABIR
Stretched... their... arms. 

MS. GABLE
Excellent job. Thank you, Kabir. 

Kabir lets out a breath. He feels the sweat on his palms. He knows he is smart. He can build a working engine from scraps. But reading aloud makes him feel like he is moving through deep mud. He has dyslexia, and it does not go away just because he tries harder. 

EXT. SCHOOL COURTYARD - DAY

Kabir and Leo sit on a concrete bench. The autumn air is cool. 

Kabir has the blueprint spread out on his lap. He uses his folding ruler to measure the distances between the lines on the paper. 

LEO
How are we going to use that? We do not even know where the door is. 

KABIR
Yes, we do. Look at the letters. 

Kabir points to a jumble of characters near the boiler room symbol. The letters are "B", "O", "I", "L", "E", "R". But they are written in a spiral. 

KABIR
They are not just words. They are a map of the floor tiles. The letters match the shape of the bricks on the wall. 

LEO
Are you sure? 

KABIR
My dyslexia makes text shift, but it also helps me see patterns in the spaces between things. The spaces here show a corridor. 

LEO
Then let's go. Before Mr. Henderson locks the basement. 

INT. BASEMENT CORRIDOR - AFTERNOON

The basement is dark. Pipes run along the ceiling like giant metal vines. The air smells of rust and old coal. 

Kabir holds a flashlight. The beam cuts through the dust. 

They walk past the giant school boiler. It hums like a sleeping beast. 

Behind the boiler is a heavy wooden door. It has a brass sign that reads: "ACCESS DENIED."

Leo pulls on the handle. It does not move. 

LEO
Locked. Of course. 

Kabir shines his light on the lock. It is not a keyhole. It is a rotating dial with letters carved into the brass. 

INT. THE GEAR GATE - CONTINUOUS

Kabir kneels before the dial. There are three rings of letters. 

LEO
Is there a code word? 

Kabir looks at the map. He looks at the dial. 

He tries to sound out the letters on the outer ring. "P-H-O-N." 

KABIR
Phon. Like phone? Or fon? 

LEO
Maybe it spells "PHONICS"? 

KABIR
No. The letters do not match. 

Kabir closes his eyes. He visualizes the sounds. He remembers a poster in the library about the school's founder, Mr. Phineas. 

KABIR
Phineas. P-H-I-N-E-A-S. 

He opens his eyes. He looks at the letters on the dial. He does not try to read them as words. He treats them as interlocking shapes. 

He clicks his folding ruler open. He aligns the ruler with the dial. 

KABIR
The letters have to share a sound. The 'ph' sound. 

He turns the first ring to 'P'. He turns the second ring to 'H'. He turns the third ring to 'O'. 

Click. 

The lock releases. The heavy door swings open. 

LEO
How did you do that? 

KABIR
I stopped trying to read the word. I just looked at how the letter shapes fit together. 

INT. THE ROTATING TUNNEL - CONTINUOUS

They step into a narrow brick tunnel. The floor is made of hexagonal tiles. 

Suddenly, a low rumble echoes. The walls begin to slide. The floor tiles shift. 

Leo grabs Kabir's arm. 

LEO
The tunnel is moving! Which way is out? 

Leo looks back. The door they entered is gone. A solid brick wall stands in its place. 

LEO
We are trapped! 

Kabir stands still. He does not panic. He closes his eyes and listens to the grinding gears behind the walls. 

He opens his folding ruler. He holds it up to the hexagonal tiles. 

KABIR
The floor is a grid. It is rotating thirty degrees every time the gears turn. 

He looks at the blueprint. The overlapping letters on the map now align perfectly with the angle of the floor tiles. 

KABIR
We need to walk on the letters that do not move. The anchors. 

LEO
Which ones are those? 

KABIR
The consonants. They hold the structure. 

Kabir steps onto a tile marked with a faded letter "T". The tile remains stable. 

He points to a tile three feet ahead, marked with an "R". 

KABIR
Jump to the R! 

Leo jumps. The tile holds. 

They move step by step, navigating the shifting floor like a giant board game. Kabir guides them, his mind mapping the three-dimensional grid with ease. 

INT. THE CHAMBER OF ECHOES - CONTINUOUS

They reach a circular room. In the center is a brass pedestal with three acoustic tubes. 

Above the tubes, three letters are carved into the stone: "B", "D", "P". 

A dusty speaker on the wall crackles to life. A recorded voice from 1954 speaks. It is deep and scratchy. 

RECORDED VOICE
To find the treasure of the past, you must follow the sound that stands alone. 

The speaker plays three distinct sounds. 

SOUND: "Bah." 

SOUND: "Dah." 

SOUND: "Pah." 

Then, a sequence of rapid sounds plays. 

SOUND: "Bah-dah-pah-dah-bah."

LEO
That was too fast. I couldn't keep track. 

KABIR
I can. 

Kabir's auditory memory is sharp. He can repeat any sound sequence he hears once. 

KABIR
It was: Bah. Dah. Pah. Dah. Bah. 

He looks at the letters on the wall. He connects the speech sounds to the letters. 

KABIR
B. D. P. D. B. 

He runs to the acoustic tubes. He presses the brass buttons below the letters in the exact order of the sounds. 

Click. Click. Click. Click. Click. 

A hidden door in the brick wall slides open. 

LEO
Your memory is amazing. 

KABIR
I have to remember sounds. I can't always rely on the letters on the page. 

INT. THE COAL VAULT - CONTINUOUS

They enter a large, dusty vault. In the corner is an old coal chute. 

In the center of the room, resting on a wooden table, is a silver steel box. It has a plaque on the front. 

Kabir approaches the box. He wipes the dust off the plaque. 

The plaque reads: "GREEN VALLEY TIME CAPSULE - 1954." 

KABIR
We found it. 

Leo tries to lift the lid. It is rusted shut. 

LEO
It won't budge. We need a crowbar. 

Kabir looks at his folding ruler. It is made of sturdy seasoned wood and brass joints. 

He folds the ruler into a tight, thick wedge. He slides the wedge under the lip of the rusted lid. 

He presses down with all his weight. 

With a loud crack, the rust breaks. The lid pops open. 

Inside the box are old black-and-white photos, a wool school pennant, and a heavy cardboard box with a picture of a crane on it. 

Kabir lifts the cardboard box. The label reads: "TITAN ERECTOR SET - GOLD STAR EDITION." 

He opens the box. Inside, resting in green velvet compartments, are shiny brass plates, miniature steel beams, and the golden brass gear. 

It is beautiful. It is heavy. It is exactly what he needs. 

LEO
You got it! 

Suddenly, a loud noise echoes from the tunnel. 

FOOTSTEPS. Heavy boots walking on the hexagonal tiles. 

MR. HENDERSON (O.S.)
Who's down here? 

LEO
(whispering)
Mr. Henderson! 

INT. THE COAL VAULT - CONTINUOUS

Leo looks back at the tunnel. 

LEO
We can't go back that way. He'll catch us. 

Kabir looks up at the coal chute. It is a steep metal slide leading up to the school courtyard. 

He measures the angle with his eyes. He visualizes the space. 

KABIR
The chute. It leads to the trash bins outside. 

LEO
It's too steep! 

KABIR
No, it is thirty-five degrees. If we climb, we can reach the grating. I can use the ruler to unlatch it. 

Kabir climbs into the chute. His sneakers grip the metal sides. He crawls upward. 

Leo follows him. 

At the top of the chute, a heavy iron grate blocks the light. 

Kabir reaches up. He slides his folded ruler through the grate. He hooks the latch and pulls. 

The grate swings open. 

They scramble out onto the concrete courtyard just as the cellar door below slams open. 

EXT. SCHOOL COURTYARD - CONTINUOUS

Kabir and Leo lie on the grass, catching their breath. 

They look at the school building. The sun is setting, casting a warm orange glow over the brick walls. 

Kabir holds the golden gear in his hand. It catches the last rays of sunlight. 

LEO
We actually did it. 

KABIR
We did. 

INT. KABIR'S WORKSHOP - NIGHT

Kabir sits at his desk. The room is lit by a single desk lamp. 

The toy crane is fully assembled. The golden brass gear is nestled perfectly in the center of the drive mechanism. 

Kabir flips the switch. 

The motor hums. The golden gear turns. The crane's arm rises smoothly, lifting a heavy wooden block off the desk. 

Kabir smiles. 

He opens his notebook to write his project label. He wants to write: "The Golden Gear Crane."

He writes "The". 

Then he stops. He struggles to connect the letters for "Golden". He writes "G-o-l-d-e-n", but the letters look uneven. He has to think hard about the spelling. He still has dyslexia. The adventure did not cure him. It did not make reading easy. 

But he does not mind. He looks at his working crane. He knows he can build anything. 

He clicks his folding ruler closed. 

He places it next to his crane. 

He is ready for the next build. 

ANDY (O.S.)
Kabir! Dinner time! 

KABIR
Coming, Mom! 

Kabir turns off the light. The golden gear glows softly in the dark. 

FADE OUT.